stem:
Given this code, predict the result of calling `Eagle().fly()`:
class Bird {
void fly() => print("Bird flying");
}
class Eagle implements Bird {
@override
void fly() => print("Eagle gliding");
}

Consider this constrained mixin code:
class Pet {
String name;
Pet(this.name);
}
mixin CanBark on Pet {
void bark() => print("$name says woof!");
}
class Dog extends Pet with CanBark {
Dog() : super("Buddy");
}
class Fish {
// want to apply "with CanBark" here
}
If class `Fish` (not extending `Pet`) tries to declare `class Fish with CanBark {}`, what happens?

Given:
class User {
final String name;
factory User.fromJson(Map j) => User._(j['name']);
User._(this.name);
}
Why is the `User._` constructor made private (with `_`)?

Given this generic class code:
class Box<T> {
T value;
Box(this.value);
void show() => print("Value = $value");
}
void main() => Box<int>(10).show();
If `Box<int>(10)` is replaced with `Box<String>(10)`, what happens?

Given this generic constraint:
abstract class Animal { String sound(); }
class Cat extends Animal { sound() => "Meow"; }
class Box<T extends Animal> {
T pet;
Box(this.pet);
void play() => print(pet.sound());
}
What does the `<T extends Animal>` syntax mean?

Given:
var base = [1, 2, 3];
var list = [
...base,
if (true) 99,
for (var x in base) x * 10
];
print(list);
What is printed?

Given this StreamController.broadcast() code:
var c = StreamController<int>.broadcast();
c.stream.listen((v) => print("A:$v"));
c.stream.listen((v) => print("B:$v"));
c..add(1)..add(2);
What is printed, in order?

Given this repository code using a Stream:
Stream<int> counter() async {
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
await Future.delayed(Duration(milliseconds: 300));
yield i;
}
}
When this Stream is bound to `StreamBuilder(stream: repo.counter(), ...)`, how will the UI behave?

A student writes this Repository:
class Repo {
Future<String> getUser() async {
await Future.delayed(Duration(milliseconds: 300));
return "Anna";
}
}
and calls `var name = repo.getUser();` (without using `await`). What type and value will variable `name` have at the moment of assignment?

Given:
return MaterialApp(
theme: ThemeData.light(useMaterial3: true),
darkTheme: ThemeData.dark(useMaterial3: true),
themeMode: ThemeMode.dark,
home: const DemoTheme(),
);
Inside a child widget, what is the correct syntax to access the current theme's style (e.g. `titleLarge`)?

This code is used to pick a date:
Future<void> pickDate() async {
final date = await showDatePicker(
context: context,
initialDate: DateTime.now(),
firstDate: DateTime(2000),
lastDate: DateTime(2100),
);
if (date != null) setState(() => selectedDate = date);
}
Why is checking `if (date != null)` needed before calling `setState`?

The lecture has this snippet:
Widget tile(String t, String s) => Card(
margin: const EdgeInsets.symmetric(vertical: 6),
child: ListTile(title: Text(t), subtitle: Text(s)),
);
What is the main benefit of extracting repeated UI into a separate function/widget like `tile()`, in the spirit of "Everything is a Widget"?

A student writes:
class DemoTheme extends StatelessWidget {
@override
Widget build(BuildContext context) {
return Scaffold(
body: Center(child: Text('Current theme', style: Theme.of(context).textTheme.titleLarge)),
);
}
}
What role does `BuildContext context` in the `build` function play when calling `Theme.of(context)`?
